","Saltatory conduction is a type of nerve impulse transmission that occurs in long nerve fibers, such as those of the central nervous system. It is characterized by the rapid transmission of the nerve impulse through the axon, with the signal jumping between gaps called **nodes of Zalisalgin**. These nodes are located along the axon wall and contain a high concentration of sodium ions, which are essential for the nerve impulse to propagate.

In contrast, non-saltatory conduction, also known as ""graded"" conduction, occurs in uncoated axons or in axons with a thin covering of connective tissue. In this type, the nerve impulse travels continuously along the axon, with the signal gradually weakening as it travels.

The main difference between saltatory and non-saltatory conduction is the speed of transmission and the role of the axon wall. In saltatory conduction, the rapid transmission is due to the signal jumping from one node to another, while in non-saltatory conduction, the signal moves continuously along the axon.

In summary, saltatory conduction is more efficient and faster than non-saltatory conduction, as it allows the nerve impulse to travel over longer distances with less energy consumption. This is due to the selective depolarization of the nodes of Zalisalgin, which allows the signal to be transmitted without significant energy loss.
