","When solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH), the following observable effects occur:
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the aqueous solution.
2. The solution becomes basic (alkaline) due to the release of hydroxide ions (OH⁻).
3. The solution becomes blue with phenolphthalein indicator (if added).
4. A white precipitate of sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) forms.

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:
\[
\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{NaOH} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2 + \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]
This shows the dissociation of benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) into their respective ions:
- Benzoic acid dissociates into H⁺ and C₇H₅O₂⁻ ions.
- Sodium hydroxide dissociates into Na⁺ and OH⁻ ions.
- The H⁺ ions react with OH⁻ ions to form water (H₂O).
- The remaining ions, Na⁺ and C₇H₅O₂⁻, combine to form sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)).

Thus, the final products are sodium benzoate and water.
