","The molecular formula C4H10O suggests that there are possible isomers of the alcohol (R-OH) functional group. There are two types of isomerism present in C4H10O: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when the atoms are connected in different orders. For C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism: chain isomerism and functional group isomerism.

   a) Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon skeleton of the molecule is different. For C4H10O, there are two chain isomers: n-butanol and isobutanol.

   - n-butanol: The carbon atoms are connected in a straight chain (CH3-CH2-CH2-CH2-OH).
   - isobutanol: The carbon atoms are connected in a branched chain (CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-OH).

   b) Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the same molecular formula can be represented by different functional groups. For C4H10O, there is one example of functional group isomerism: butan-2-one (CH3-CO-CH2-CH3).

2. Optical isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which means it has four different groups attached to a carbon atom. In C4H10O, only isobutanol has a chiral center (the carbon atom bonded to the hydroxyl group and two methyl groups). Therefore, isobutanol exists as a pair of optical isomers, which are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism in a molecule with the chemical formula C4H10O are structural isomerism (chain isomerism and functional group isomerism) and optical isomerism.
