For the reaction:

2 CO(g) + O2(g) → 2 CO2(g)

The standard entropies (S°) at 298 K are:

S°(CO) = 197.6 J/(mol·K)
S°(O2) = 205.1 J/(mol·K)
S°(CO2) = 213.8 J/(mol·K)

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy for the reaction:

ΔS = [2 × S°(CO2)] - [2 × S°(CO) + S°(O2)]

ΔS = [2 × 213.8 J/(mol·K)] - [2 × 197.6 J/(mol·K) + 205.1 J/(mol·K)]

ΔS = (427.6 J/(mol·K)) - (792.2 J/(mol·K))

ΔS = -364.6 J/(mol·K)

Since we have 3 moles of CO in the reaction, we need to multiply the change in entropy by 3:

ΔS = -364.6 J/(mol·K) × 3

ΔS = -1093.8 J/K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -1093.8 J/K.
