1. TiO2 nanoparticles: Graphene oxide ratio: The general guideline is to maintain a ratio of 10:1 to 100:1 (TiO2:GO). This is because TiO2 is the primary electron transport layer, and GO is added to enhance the performance of the solar cell.

2. Optimal ratio: Studies have shown that a ratio of 50:1 (TiO2:GO) or 70:1 (TiO2:GO) can provide the best trade-between between efficiency and stability. This ratio provides sufficient TiO2 to provide efficient charge transport while maintaining the optimal amount of GO to enhance the performance of the solar cell.

The ratio of TiO2 and GO affects the overall efficiency and stability of the solar cell in the following ways:

1. Efficiency: The addition of GO to the TiO2 nanoparticles can improve the efficiency of the solar cell by enhancing the light absorption, charge separation, and charge transport properties of the active layer. This is due to the unique electronic and structural properties of GO, which can facilitate the transfer of electrons from the dye to the TiO2 nanoparticles.

2. Stability: The addition of GO can also improve the stability of the solar cell by reducing the recombination of the photogenerated electron-hole pairs. This is because GO can act as an electron transfer layer, which can help to prevent the recombination of the photogenerated electron-hole pairs at the TiO2/dye interface.

However, it is important to note that the addition of GO to the TiO2 nanoparticles can also lead to some challenges, such as the agglomeration of the nanoparticles and the formation of defects in the active layer. Therefore, it is essential to carefully optimize the synthesis method and the ratio of TiO2 and GO to achieve the best performance of the solar cell.
