","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis that allows the production of a single enantiomer (or enantiomerically pure compound) with a known absolute configuration. This means that the absolute configuration of the product is known and determined without relying on comparison with a known compound. The other type of asymmetric synthesis is relative asymmetric synthesis, where the configuration of the product is determined relative to a known compound.

In contrast, in other types of asymmetric synthesis, the configuration of the product is determined relative to a known compound, such as a known chiral auxiliary or a known chiral catalyst. This method is less information-specific because the absolute configuration of the product is not explicitly determined.

For example, in the synthesis of (R)-2-butanol, if the product is obtained by asymmetric hydrogenation of 2-butanone using a chiral catalyst, the configuration of the product is determined relative to the chiral catalyst. In this case, the absolute configuration of the product is not known without additional information about the chiral catalyst.

In contrast, if the same product (R)-2-butanol is obtained through asymmetric synthesis using a chiral auxiliary, the absolute configuration of the product can be determined by comparing the optical rotation of the product to the known optical rotation of the chiral auxiliary. This method is called absolute asymmetric synthesis.

In summary, the main difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis is that in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration of the product is determined without relying on comparison with a known compound. This is achieved by using a chiral auxiliary or a chiral catalyst with a known absolute configuration.
