","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickSort in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If the input list \( n \) has only one element, it is already sorted, so no comparisons are needed (\( C(1) = 0 \)).
2. **Intermediate Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the algorithm splits the input list into two equal-sized sublists, each of size \(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor\) and \(\lceil n/2 \rceil\).
   - The first term \( C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor \) represents the number of comparisons in the first sublist.
   - The second term \( C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil \) represents the number of comparisons in the second sublike.
   - The third term \( n - 1 \) accounts for the single comparison used to compare the pivot element with each element in its subleaves. This is because the pivot is compared to \( n - 1 \) elements to determine its position.
3. **Putting it Together**: The total number of comparisons is the sum of the comparisons in the two subleaves plus the one for the pivot.

Thus, the recurrence relation is:
\[
C(n) = C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil + n - 1.
\]
This recurrence is derived from the Big-O notation of Big-O(n log n), which is the average time complexity of QuickSuit. The time required to run QuickSuit is proportional to the number of comparisons required.
