","The mass and composition of a star play a crucial role in determining its ultimate outcome after gravitational collapse, as per general relativity. The mass of the star is the primary factor, with the composition playing a secondary role. The mass determines the strength of the gravitational forces, which in turn affects the outcome of the collapse.

1. Low-mass stars (up to 10-20 times the mass of the Sun) end up as neutron stars. When these stars reach the end of their life cycle, they undergo a supernova explosion, leaving behind a dense core. The pressure from the strong nuclear force prevents further compression, and the core becomes a neutron star, consisting of protons and neutrons.

2. Medium-mass stars (20-40 times the mass of the Sun) also undergo supernovas, but their cores become neutron stars. However, the neutron star may become a ""neutron star cluster"" or a ""neutron star ring"" due to the higher mass and pressure.

3. High-mass stars (over 40 times the mass of the Sun) experience a more catastrophic collapse, leading to the formation of a black matter. The gravitational forces are so intense that they overcome even the strong nuclear force, causing the core to collapse into a single point called a singularity. The star's surface collapses into a dense, infinitely-gravity well, forming a black hole.

The composition of the star also plays a role, though to a lesser extent. A higher iron content in the star's core can lead to a more violent and more efficient supernova, increasing the likelihood of a black-state formation.

In summary, the mass of a star determines its ultimate outcome after gravitational collapse, with larger stars being more likely to form black holes and smaller stars becoming neutron stars. The composition of the star can influence the outcome, but the mass is the primary factor.
