<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we use the time dilation formula:

\[
t_{\text{source}} = \frac{t_{\text{reference}}}{\sqrt{1 - \left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^2}}
\]

Where:
- \( t_{\text{source}} \) = Time taken for the moving body (cargo bag),
- \( t_{\text{reference}} \) = Time taken for the stationary point (classboard),
- \( v \) = Speed of the moving body (speed of the car),
- \( c \) = Speed of light.

Given:
- \( t_{\text{reference}} = 3 \) years (cockard clock),
- \( v = 0.6c \),
- \( c = c \) (speed of light).

Substitute the values into the time dilation formula:

\[
t_{\text{cargo}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6)^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the clock in the cargo bag will show a time of \(\boxed{3.75 \text{ years}}\) when the ship returns to Earth.

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

