A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The contribution to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) due to hard particles is given by the Obert-Cockley equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{G b}{2 d} \left( \frac{1 - \phi}{\phi} \right)
\]  
where:  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,  
- \(d\) is the diameter of the hard particles,  
- \(\phi\) is the volume fraction of the hard particles.  

Given:  
- \(G = 26 \times 10^9\) GN m\(^{-2}\) = \(26 \times 10^9\) N m\(^{-2}\)  
- \(b = 0.286\) nm = \(2.86 \times 10^{-10}\) m  
- \(d = 10^{-8}\) m  

Assuming the particles are randomly distributed, the volume fraction \(\phi\) is approximately:  
\[
\phi \approx \frac{d^3}{6^3 (d/2)^3} = \frac{1}{6^3} = \frac{1}{216} \approx 0.0046
\]  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 2.86 \times 10^{-10}}{2 \times 10^{-8}} \left( \frac{1 - 0.0046}{0.0046} \right)
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \frac{7.4156 \times 10^9}{2 \times 10^{-8}} \left( \frac{0.9954}{0.0046} \right)
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 3.7078 \times 10^{17} \times 218.57
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = 7.92 \times 10^{19} \text{ N m}^{-2} = 7.92 \text{ GPa}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{7.92 \text{ GPa}}\).
