","The net chemical reaction for the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 4\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

**Explanation**:
- **Reactants**: The battery consists of two electrodes: a lead (Pb) electrode and a lead dioxide (PbO₂) electrode. The electrolyte is an aqueous solution of sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄), which provides the H⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions.
- **Products**: During discharge, the lead and lead dioxide electrodes react with the H⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions to form lead sulfate (PbSO₄) on both electrodes. Water (H₂O) is also produced as a byproduct.
- **Net Reaction**: The net reaction is the sum of the two half-reactions:
  1. At the anode (Pb electrode): \(\text{Pb(s)} + \text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}^+(aq)\)
  2. At the cathode (PbO₂ electrode): \(\text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + \text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)}\)
  Combining these two half-reactions gives the net reaction above.

Thus, during discharge, the lead and lead dioxide electrodes are reduced to lead sulfate, and the electrolyte is depleted of H⁺ and SO₄²⁻ ions.
