","The reflectivity \( R \) of a surface is given by the ratio of the power density of the reflected light to the power density of the incident light. The reflectivity is given by:
\[
R = \frac{I_{\text{refmitted}}}{I_{\text{incident}}}
\]
The incident light intensity \( I \) is related to the absorption cross-section \( \sigma \), the number density of atoms \( n \), and the speed of light \( c \) by:
\[
I = \sigma n c
\]
The absorption cross-section \( \sigma \) is related to the Einstein coefficients \( A \) and \( B \) by:
\[
\sigma = \frac{B_21}{c}
\]
The reflectivity \( R \) is thus:
\[
R = \frac{I_{\text{refmitted}}}{I_{\text{incident}}} = \frac{\sigma n c (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT})}{I} = \frac{B_{21}}{c} n c (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT})
\]
The term \( (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT}) \) represents the absorption coefficient, which depends on the frequency of the incident light \( \nu \), the temperature \( T \), and Planck's constant \( h \). The saturation intensity \( I_s \) is defined as the intensity at which the absorption cross-section \( \sigma \) becomes constant, i.e., \( \sigma = \sigma_{\text{sat}} \). The reflectivity \( R \) can also be expressed in terms of the saturation intensity \( I_s \):
\[
R = \frac{B_{21}}{c} n c (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT}) = \frac{B_{21}}{c} n c (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT}) \frac{I_s}{I_s} = \frac{B_{21}}{c} n c \frac{I_s}{I_s} (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT})
\]
Thus, the final expression for the reflectivity \( R \) in terms of the Einstein coefficients \( A \) and \( B \), the intensity \( I \), and the saturation intensity \( I_s \) is:
\[
R = \frac{B_{21}}{c} n c \frac{I_s}{I_s} (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT})
\]
This expression shows that the reflectivity depends on the Einstein coefficients, the number density of atoms, the frequency of the incident light, the temperature, and the saturation intensity. The reflectivity is also a function of the incident light intensity, as the absorption coefficient \( (1 - e^{-h\nu / kT}) \) depends on the intensity.
