","To determine the reaction times that can be followed, we need to calculate the residence time of the solution in the flow tube. The residence time is the average time for a drop of solution to travel from the mixing chamber to the observation point.

1. Calculate the cross-sectional area of the tubing:
Diameter = 0.1 mm, so radius = 0.05 mm = 50 μm
Area (A) = πr² = π(50 × 10⁻⁶ m)² ≈ 7.85 × 10⁻⁸ m²

2. Calculate the flow velocity:
Flow rate (Q) = 10 cm³/min = 1.67 × 10⁻⁶ m³/s
Velocity (v) = Q / A = (1.67 × 10⁻⁶ m³/s) / (7.85 × 10⁻⁸ m²) ≈ 21.9 m/s

3. Calculate the residence time (τ) for different tube lengths (L):
τ = L / v

For a 1 cm tube:
τ = (0.01 m) / (21.9 m/s) ≈ 4.62 × 10⁻⁴ s ≈ 462 μs

For a 2 cm tube:
τ = (0.02 m) / (21.9 m/s) ≈ 9.24 × 10⁻⁴ s ≈ 924 μs

For a 3 cm tube:
τ = (0.03 m) / (21.9 m/s) ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻³ s ≈ 1.38 ms

And so on, up to a 10 cm tube.

The reaction times that can be followed depend on the rate of the reaction. If the reaction is very fast, the time required for the reaction to complete will be much shorter than the residence time, and the entire tube can be considered a single-mixed region. However, if the reaction is slow, the residence time will be an important factor, and the time required for the reaction to complete will be longer than the residence time. In this case, the tube can be divided into multiple mixed regions, and the reaction time can be calculated for each region.
