In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a two-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \right)\mathbf{i} + \left( \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} \right)\mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), the components along the \(z\)-axis are zero (\(V_z = 0\)). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( 0 - 0 \right)\mathbf{i} + \left( 0 - 0 \right)\mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)\mathbf{k}
\]
Thus, the curl is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)\mathbf{k}
\]
The curl of \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a vector along the \(z\)-axis, with magnitude equal to the difference of the partial derivatives of \(V_y\) with respect to \(x\) and \(V_x\) with respect to \(y\). 

Final answer: \(\boxed{\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right)\mathbf{k}}\)
