</USER_REQUEST>

<GROUND_TRUTH>
The total entropy increase of the combined system is calculated as follows:

1. **Entropy change of the water**:
   The water absorbs heat at a constant temperature of 220°C (493 K). The entropy increase of the water is:
   \[
   \Delta S_{\text{water}} = \frac{\text{Latent heat absorbed}}{T} = \frac{1858.5}{493} = 3.77 \text{ kJ/kg-K}.
   \]

2. **Entropy change of the gas**:
   The gas is cooled from 1100°C (1373 K) to 550°C (823 K). The heat transferred from the gas is equal to the latent heat absorbed by the water (1858.5 kJ). The mass flow rate of the gas (\(\dot{m}_g\)) is determined as:
   \[
